WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.590
Now, you may be asking, "How does

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.190
memory.md actually get filled up?" Well,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.350
the good news is with the latest

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.110
versions of cloud code, it actually

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.870
fills itself up automatically. So,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.350
whenever you talk to cloud code and it

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.990
deems like it is necessary to keep that

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.110
conversation in its memory, it will do

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.950
so. So, you may find that even without

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.390
you prompting it, it will write that

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.950
back to its memory.md on its own. Now,

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.750
apart from writing it itself, you can

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.830
also steer and just ask cloud code to

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.430
remember a particular nugget or a

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.390
particular piece of information about

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:29.510
you and it will write it to your

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.830
memory.md just from that natural

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:33.790
language command. And obviously, you can

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.630
also edit it yourself, although with a

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.350
lot of these agentic platforms, I

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.190
actually advise against that because it

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.470
is just much more efficient for you to

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.470
just have your agent review and edit

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.710
those files in markdown text on your

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.110
behalf. Now, auto memory logging is on

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.230
by default if you are on the latest

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:51.870
version of cloud code, but if you do

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.950
want to check it, if you are on VS code,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.950
if you type in {slash} memory, what it

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:57.630
will prompt you to do is to continue in

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.230
the terminal, which if you click on

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.910
that, that will just open up the

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.910
terminal view for you and it will open

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.030
up cloud code for you from this terminal

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:07.190
view. And you can see here, if I go up,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.630
I can actually toggle auto memory as off

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.670
by clicking [snorts] on enter or on,

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.280
which should be there by default.
